{"kind":"request","text":"CE-2: a custody record of a caller with no replica ends. Found by\nverdict 10e67581, note 1, on the client note amendment (43de81ee).\n\nScene: an agent on the MCP service sends 256 requests whose replies\nare lost. Each signing expires, and the journal holds none of the\nkeys. Each record stays unresolved. The agent's next request is\nrefused `full`, reason `count`, for good.\n\nThe fact: section 5.5 of `notes/2026-11-13-scope-client.md` ends such\na record through `absent`, which only a caller with a replica calls.\nThe MCP service, the hosted agent and the CLI install hold no\nreplica. Their records with a dead latest signing never end.\n\nBefore code, send one act that answers:\n\n1. For each of the three callers, what bounds its unresolved records\n   today, and whether a store rule already removes an old record.\n2. The proof that the caller can state. My proposal: a session read\n   of `log` to the head after the signing dies, checked as\n   `ScopeHandle.log` checks it, and then `absent`. A `not-found` of\n   `settle` alone ends nothing, as section 5.5 says.\n3. What `settle` and the act tools answer for a record that ended\n   with `unsent`. The answer shapes of NF-2 stay as they are, or the\n   act names each shape that changes.\n\nOrder: low. It lands before hosted MCP opens for a room whose members\nthe operator does not know, with af11896e. It follows C3, which\nbuilds the outcome `unsent`. One head, one gate.","body":{"conditions":"An act before code answers the three points. One head with one gate, after C3. A test: 257 lost and expired requests in one MCP store, and the 257th request signs. No answer shape of NF-2 changes without a ruling.","target_head":"a200cd0a5999852eb9142136d2b11e2b7c87e02b","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","target_repo":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}